    {
      "url": "https://youtu.be/L3iKvwoXZ38",
      "title": "Midway Atoll NWR Wildlife Live Camera",
      "year": 2025,
      "duration": 0,
      "poster": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/L3iKvwoXZ38/maxresdefault.jpg",
      "desc": "Welcome to the Kuaihelani (Midway Atoll) live cam. This live cam is brought to you by the Friends of Midway Atoll (FOMA), which is a nonprofit dedicated to supporting the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service in their management of the Midway Atoll National Wildlife Refuge and Battle of Midway National Memorial. As such, FOMA established this live camera to “bring the place to the people,” which is one of the refuge’s goals. FOMA members and donors made this live cam possible, which is located near the center of Sand Island facing N/NE toward the lagoon.   Please consider becoming a member or donating to FOMA to keep this camera broadcasting and to support conservation of wildlife on Midway Atoll National Wildlife Refuge.  https://friendsofmidway.org/YouTube-donation-membership  FOMA's Online Store: https://fomastore.printful.me/  Curious about the birds you're seeing and hearing? Check out FOMA's full list: https://friendsofmidway.org/explore/  You will now be able to enjoy the full breeding cycle of the world’s largest breeding colony of mōlī (Laysan albatross) and ka‘upu (black-footed albatross). Starting in late October or early November, adult albatross will begin returning to Kuaihelani to reunite with their life-long mates. During this time, courtship will abound with mates reuniting after their absence from the island for several months and soon thereafter copulating and building a nest for their single egg. Mated pairs will soon settle into the exhausting effort of egg laying, incubation and then feeding their growing chicks until their eventual dispersal (fledging) to sea from late June to early August. Soon after the mated pairs arrive and begin nesting, single, mostly younger birds arrive and spend months dancing and courting others in hopes of finding their forever mate. Most of these birds return to the same vicinity where they were raised and will spend their lives breeding in the same neighborhood as their parents.   You will see mōlī across the view shed and in the distance you will see scattered black-footed albatross, but you may also see at any time the endangered koloa pōhaka (Laysan duck), manu-o-Kū (white tern), kōlea (Pacific golden-plover), ʻakekeke (ruddy turnstone) and koaʻe ʻula (red-tailed tropicbirds) in the sky doing their magnificent aerial mating dance. After the albatross leave the island, we will reset the camera so that you may enjoy the nesting of white terns and other birds until the eventual return of the albatross.    And don’t miss the evening spectacle as the nunulu (Bonin petrels) arrive by the thousands to attend to their nest sites in excavated underground burrows.  NOTE: Midway Atoll is over 1,000 miles northwest of Honolulu, HI and is on Samoa Standard Time so from the East Coast there could be a 6 to 7 hour difference from the East Coast.    Midway Atoll is a National Wildlife Refuge and, therefore, it is a policy to minimize human interference with nature. As a result, you may witness both the joy and hardships of life. The camera is not a monitoring tool and is not monitored by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. Instead, FOMA's goal for the camera is to bring to you a glimpse into the larger Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Monument, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site of cultural and biological significance.    Visit us at:https://friendsofmidway.org/ and follow us on social media: https://www.facebook.com/FriendsofMidwayNWR https://www.instagram.com/friendsofmidwaynwr/ https://bsky.app/profile/friendsofmidway.bsky.social  We also encourage you to visit: The Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Monument: https://www.papahanaumokuakea.gov/  The Papahānaumokuākea Native Hawaiian Cultural Working Group (CWG): https://www.alohacwg.com/   The CWG is composed of Native Hawaiians who have deep connections and historical ties to Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Marine Monument through a living pilina (relationship) bound by genealogy, cultural protocol, research, and stewardship. Here is a direct link to an informational flyer that explains the meaning of native Hawaiian names: https://www.alohacwg.com/nomenclature-library"
